Enable the xHCI host the three M.2 Key-B slots hang off.

The SoC dtsi ships both USB controllers disabled and the board DTS never turned
one on, so /sys/bus/usb/devices was empty and none of the three installed 5G
modules could be seen -- which looks exactly like a modem, SIM or antenna fault
and is neither. There was no USB host at all.

ssusb1 is the right one: it takes the *_CK_P1 clocks, and the schematic
(BPI-R4Pro-V10-SCH-20250806, "USB3.2 HUB" / "M.2 KEY B(5G) x3") routes SSUSB_P1
into the VL822-Q7 hub feeding all three M.2 slots and the Type-A socket. ssusb0
is muxed with PCIE_1L_0 and is a different path.

Slot power, voltage select and reset are on the PCA9555 at i2c1 0x20 and are not
part of this patch.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro-8x.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro-8x.dts
@@ -456,3 +456,24 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/* USB host for the three M.2 Key-B slots.
+ *
+ * The SoC dtsi leaves both xHCI controllers disabled and the board DTS never
+ * turned one on, so /sys/bus/usb/devices was empty and no module could be seen
+ * at all -- not a modem, SIM or antenna problem, there was simply no host.
+ *
+ * ssusb1 is the one: it takes the *_CK_P1 clocks, and the schematic routes
+ * SSUSB_P1 into the VL822-Q7 USB 3.2 hub that feeds all three M.2 Key-B slots
+ * and the Type-A socket. ssusb0 is muxed with PCIE_1L_0 and is not this path.
+ *
+ * Slot power (BKEY-x-PDN), voltage select (BKEY-x-Vset) and reset (BKEY-x-RST)
+ * hang off the PCA9555 at i2c1 0x20; they are handled separately.
+ */
+&ssusb1 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&tphy {
+	status = "okay";
+};
